# doors — the great bronze south door, its pedimented doorcase, and the side doors east and west
#
# Everything here is cut into the SOUTH FACE OF THE MAIN BLOCK, z = -7.50, standing on the podium
# floor at y = 1.80, inside the portico. Nothing in this file needs to know anything about the
# wall it pierces except where its face is: the openings are punched 1.80 m northward, which is
# deeper than any wall this building can plausibly have, and the manifest takes them away at the
# end. Add a wing beside this next month without reading a line of it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD: bronze, and a subtracted detail on a curved surface.
#
# The building's rough metal lives here and almost nowhere else: 2.70 x 4.50 m of it on the centre
# line, in the deepest shadow in the scene, at the one place a viewer walks up to. `bronze` is
# rough=110 metal=190 — the only brushed metal in the palette that covers an area rather than a
# moulding, so it is what decides whether the path tracer's rough-metal lobe reads as metal at all
# when the only light on it is bounce off a limestone soffit.
#
# THE REAL TEST IS THE PANELS. The great door's leaves are not flat. Their outer face is a cylinder
# of radius 5.40 (12M) struck from z = -2.25, so the pair bows 0.171 m out of its frame at the
# centre line and has fallen back inside its own reveal by the jamb. Every detail on it — four sunk
# panels to a leaf, a bead round each, a gilt patera in each, the meeting joint — is cut or raised
# ALONG THAT CURVE, so no arris in the door is axis-aligned, none is voxel-aligned, and every voxel
# along every one of them is a partial voxel. The depths, against a 0.03125 voxel at the contract's
# metre 32:
#
#     first step    0.0225   under one voxel
#     panel floor   0.0450   one and a half
#     patera face   0.0225   standing back out of the floor
#     bead          0.0450   standing proud, 0.045 across, 61 m of it over the three doors
#     meeting joint 0.045 wide by 0.045 deep, 4.50 m of it, down the centre line
#
# The manifest then displaces THE WHOLE BUILDING by 12 mm of `grain_fine` — a third of a voxel and
# a quarter of the panel's own depth. That is the test: a displacement moves a surface along a
# noise field that knows nothing about which arris it is crossing, so a 45 mm step under a 12 mm
# swell either survives as carving or dissolves into a dented sheet.
#
# WHAT IT ACTUALLY FOUND, and it was not what this file was written expecting. The arrises survive
# the displacement perfectly well. What does not survive is a SUBTRACTION whose cutter stops at the
# surface it cuts, or a raised moulding given a surface of its own rather than the parent's. Both
# leave the field reporting a distance to a surface that is not there any more, and 12 mm of noise
# then resurrects it, one loose voxel at a time. Written the obvious way this part came out of the
# sampler in 129 pieces with 433 voxels adrift; written the way it is now it is one piece with
# none, at the same dimensions, with nothing visibly different about it. The three rules that got
# it there are written where each of them applies — over the panels, over the fasciae, and over
# the bead — and they are the most useful thing in this file.
#
# THE SIDE DOORS ARE THE CONTROL AND THAT IS WHY THEY ARE FLAT. Same bronze, same depths, same
# bead, same grain — panels 0.54 x 0.81 cut into a plane instead of a cylinder, 3.6 m away in the
# same frame under the same light. They are how you tell a fault of the curve from a fault of the
# depth, and the answer above came out of exactly that comparison: the flat doors never shed a
# voxel, which is what pointed at the construction rather than at the curvature. Do not "improve"
# them by bowing them too; that throws the comparison away.
#
# DO NOT DEEPEN THE PANELS to make them survive. 0.045 is M/10 and it is chosen to sit just above
# the grain, not comfortably above it. Do not `weather` anything in this file either: weathering
# is a second displacement, and a second displacement on top of the first is precisely what would
# make this measurement meaningless.
#
# The gilt paterae are one more thing on purpose: a 0.27 m disc of gilt (rough=64 metal=225,
# polished) inside a 0.90 m panel of bronze (rough=110 metal=190, brushed), on the same curve,
# under the same light, 0.0225 apart in depth. Two metals that differ only in roughness, close
# enough together to be compared in one glance rather than in two frames.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. Every number below is one of those or a simple fraction of it:
# 0.225 = M/2, 0.18 = 0.4M, 0.135 = 0.3M, 0.09 = M/5, 0.045 = M/10, 0.0225 = M/20. The great door
# is 3D wide by 10M tall; its case is 9M wide over the cornice and its apex is at 8.10 = 18M. The
# side doorcases run from x = 4.05 to x = 6.75 — the outer column axes of the portico, exactly.
# The curvature radius 5.40 = 12M, and the numbers that fall out of it (the leaf face standing at
# z = -7.4785 at the jamb) are construction, not dimension.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE DOORCASE PLINTH — y 1.71 to 2.25, x -6.75 to 6.75
# ================================================================================================
#
# One band at the foot of all three doorcases, which is what makes this part one piece of stone
# rather than three. Its lower 0.09 (y 1.71 to 1.80) runs UNDER the openings, buried in the podium,
# and is the threshold every leaf laps 0.05 into; its upper 0.45 is the plinth block the three
# architraves stand on, and the openings cut it away where the doorways are.
#
# It ends at x = +-6.75, on the outer column axes, so it reads as belonging to the portico's bays.

let doors_plinth_band = box -6.75 1.71 -7.68   6.75 2.16 -7.35 round=0.018

# The congé off the plinth into the wall: full width at the bottom, died into the face at the top.
# First corner in the stone — at the wall, at the bottom — second in the air.
let doors_plinth_cap = cavetto -6.75 2.16 -7.50   6.75 2.25 -7.725 run=x

let doors_plinth = union { doors_plinth_band doors_plinth_cap }

# ================================================================================================
# THE GREAT DOOR — the opening, 2.70 (3D) by 4.50 (10M), on the centre line
# ================================================================================================
#
# x -1.35 .. 1.35, y 1.80 .. 6.30. The width is the portico's central intercolumniation exactly:
# the door jambs stand on the axes of the two middle columns.

# --- the architrave: three fasciae, each stepping forward of the one below --------------------
#
#   fascia one    0.180 wide (0.4M), standing 0.045 proud of the wall
#   astragal      a 0.045 roll on the joint between them
#   fascia two    0.135 wide (0.3M), 0.090 proud
#   fascia three  0.135 wide (0.3M), 0.135 proud
#                 0.450 = M in all
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EACH FASCIA IS TWO JAMBS AND A HEAD UNIONED, AND NOT A BOX MINUS A BOX.
#
# A subtracted frame is the obvious way to write this: `difference { outer inner }` mitres the two
# corners perfectly, is three lines, and makes exactly the same solid. It also leaves loose voxels
# once the manifest's 12 mm of grain goes on. `doors_gd_f3` written that way came out of the
# sampler in THREE PIECES, with single voxels hanging in the reveal a few centimetres under the
# architrave's soffit. Written as three boxes unioned it is one piece, and the two differ by
# nothing you can see.
#
# What makes it certain rather than a hunch: the loose voxels did not move when the arris round was
# changed from 0.012 to 0.018 — a 6 mm change that moves every surface involved past them on both
# sides. Voxels that ignore the geometry are not being decided by the geometry.
#
# So: NO SUBTRACTIONS in the doorcase. A frame is bars, a roll is bars grown, and the only
# subtraction left in this file is the one that sinks the panels, which is written so that the cut
# reaches clear of the surface — see the note over the leaves.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

let doors_gd_f1_jamb = box  1.35 2.16 -7.545   1.53 6.48 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_f1_head = box -1.53 6.30 -7.545   1.53 6.48 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_f1 = union { mirror { doors_gd_f1_jamb } axis=x   doors_gd_f1_head }

let doors_gd_f2_jamb = box  1.53  2.16 -7.59   1.665 6.615 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_f2_head = box -1.665 6.48 -7.59   1.665 6.615 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_f2 = union { mirror { doors_gd_f2_jamb } axis=x   doors_gd_f2_head }

let doors_gd_f3_jamb = box  1.665 2.16  -7.635   1.80 6.75 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_f3_head = box -1.80  6.615 -7.635   1.80 6.75 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_f3 = union { mirror { doors_gd_f3_jamb } axis=x   doors_gd_f3_head }

# The astragal. Flat strips of no thickness at all, grown by their own radius — which is what a
# roll IS, and it turns the head-to-jamb corner without a mitre because there is no section to
# mitre. 0.045 across: one and a half voxels, running 11.7 m round the opening.
let doors_gd_ast_jamb = box 1.5075 2.16   -7.5675   1.53 6.48 -7.5675
let doors_gd_ast_head = box -1.53  6.4575 -7.5675   1.53 6.48 -7.5675
let doors_gd_ast_plate = union { mirror { doors_gd_ast_jamb } axis=x   doors_gd_ast_head }
let doors_gd_astragal = round { doors_gd_ast_plate } by=0.0225

# --- the frieze, flush with the third fascia ---------------------------------------------------
let doors_gd_frieze = box -1.80 6.66 -7.635   1.80 7.20 -7.35 round=0.018

# --- the cornice, y 7.20 to 7.65 (M), with a dentil band ---------------------------------------
#
#   bed ovolo    7.200 - 7.290   springing from the frieze face out to the dentil ground
#   dentils      7.290 - 7.470   the order's own dentil, 0.090 wide on a 0.135 pitch
#   dentil cap   7.470 - 7.515
#   corona       7.515 - 7.650   projecting 0.45 = M, and no crowning cyma because the raking
#                                cornice of the pediment takes it over, which is the rule

let doors_gd_bed      = ovolo -2.025 7.29 -7.635   2.025 7.20 -7.755 run=x
let doors_gd_bed_back = box   -2.025 7.19 -7.635   2.025 7.30 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_dback    = box   -2.025 7.25 -7.77    2.025 7.47 -7.35 round=0.018
# The dentil's ground stands 0.030 proud of where the dentil's back sits, which is one whole voxel
# of overlap. Set flush at first, and the grain shook a handful of them loose.
let doors_gd_dentil   = translate { dentil_band } 0 7.29 -7.74
let doors_gd_dentils  = repeat { doors_gd_dentil } x=0.135 nx=14
let doors_gd_dcap     = box   -2.025 7.44  -7.905  2.025 7.515 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_gd_corona   = box   -2.025 7.485 -7.95   2.025 7.65  -7.35 round=0.018

# --- the pediment, span 4.05 (9M), rise 4.05/9 = 0.45 (M) by the contract -----------------------
#
# A pediment is two rakes and a level, so it is written as two rakes and a level: the intersection
# of a box with the half spaces under the two slopes. Convex, and every one of the three is an
# exact distance, which matters — see below.
#
# THE RAKE'S NORMAL IS (RISE, HALF SPAN) = (0.45, 2.025), both module numbers, and `plane`
# normalises it, so the only derived number here is `at`: (0.45 x 0 + 2.025 x 8.10) / |0.45,2.025|
# = 16.4025 / 2.0744035 = 7.9070922, which is the apex (0, 8.10) and the corner (2.025, 7.65)
# written as one equation. That is construction, not dimension, in the same sense as the shaft's
# entasis radius, and moving the pediment means moving `at` and nothing else.
#
# TWO CONSTRUCTIONS WERE TRIED BEFORE THIS ONE AND BOTH WERE WRONG, so do not "simplify" it back:
#
#   `wedge` alone gave the VALLEY. It keeps the side ABOVE its diagonal — its triangle is the
#   upright edge, the top edge and the hypotenuse between them — so the pediment came off the
#   contact sheet as a flat band with a notch bitten out of the middle.
#
#   `difference { box wedge }` gave the right shape and 324 FLOATING VOXELS once the manifest's
#   12 mm of grain went on: a scatter of stone in mid air along y = 7.906, half a metre outside the
#   tympanum, in a part that was one clean component undisplaced. A subtracted wedge reports a
#   distance the sampler cannot skip on safely when a displacement is asking how far the surface
#   can have moved; three exact half spaces do. Whatever the fix in the engine turns out to be, a
#   pediment does not need a subtraction and should not have one.
let doors_ped_rake_e = plane  0.45 2.025 0 at=7.9070922
let doors_ped_rake_w = plane -0.45 2.025 0 at=7.9070922
let doors_ped_gable  = intersection {
    box -2.025 7.65 -9   2.025 8.10 9
    doors_ped_rake_e doors_ped_rake_w
}

# The same gable slid straight down twice. The band between two parallel rakes is the raking
# cornice; what is left inside is the tympanum; the three tile the gable exactly. The bands are
# measured VERTICALLY, 0.090 and 0.135, which on a 1-in-9 rake is 0.088 and 0.132 on the square —
# and they have to be that shallow, because the whole pediment only rises 0.45 and a raking cornice
# any deeper leaves no tympanum to see.
let doors_ped_b = translate { doors_ped_gable } 0 -0.09  0
let doors_ped_c = translate { doors_ped_gable } 0 -0.135 0

let doors_ped_corona = intersection {
    difference { doors_ped_gable doors_ped_b }
    box -2.025 7.65 -7.905   2.025 8.10 -7.35
}
let doors_ped_bed = intersection {
    difference { doors_ped_gable doors_ped_c }
    box -2.025 7.65 -7.815   2.025 8.10 -7.35
}
let doors_ped_tympanum = intersection {
    doors_ped_c
    box -2.025 7.47 -7.635   2.025 8.10 -7.35
}

let doors_gd_case = union {
    doors_gd_f1 doors_gd_f2 doors_gd_f3 doors_gd_astragal
    doors_gd_frieze
    doors_gd_bed doors_gd_bed_back doors_gd_dback doors_gd_dentils
    doors_gd_dcap doors_gd_corona
    doors_ped_corona doors_ped_bed doors_ped_tympanum
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE GREAT DOOR'S LEAVES — a pair of bronze leaves, bowed
# ================================================================================================
#
# The outer face is a cylinder of radius 5.40 (12M) about the vertical line x = 0, z = -2.25, so it
# crosses the centre line at z = -7.65 — 0.15 proud of the wall — and has fallen back to z =
# -7.4785 at the jamb, inside its own reveal. The pair bows 0.171 out of the frame. The inner face
# is the same cylinder 0.135 (0.3M) smaller, so the leaf is of one thickness everywhere.
#
# Each leaf laps 0.045 behind its jamb and 0.05 down into the threshold, which is what holds all of
# this to the doorcase — and is also just what a door does.
let doors_gd_cyl_o = cylinder 0 4.05 -2.25 r=5.40   h=9.6 axis=y
let doors_gd_cyl_a = cylinder 0 4.05 -2.25 r=5.3775 h=9.6 axis=y
let doors_gd_cyl_b = cylinder 0 4.05 -2.25 r=5.355  h=9.6 axis=y
let doors_gd_cyl_i = cylinder 0 4.05 -2.25 r=5.265  h=9.6 axis=y

let doors_gd_slab = intersection {
    difference { doors_gd_cyl_o doors_gd_cyl_i }
    box 0 1.75 -7.95   1.395 6.30 -7.20
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A PANEL IS CUT BY A PLAN MINUS A CYLINDER, AND NOT BY A PLAN INTERSECTED WITH A SHELL.
#
# The two make the same solid and only one of them survives the manifest's displacement. This cost
# a whole afternoon and it is the most useful thing in this file, so it is written down.
#
# Cut with a shell that hugs the leaf — the 0.045 of stone between r = 5.40 and r = 5.355 — the
# geometry is right and the clip comes out of the sampler in 95 pieces, with a scatter of loose
# bronze voxels hanging THREE VOXELS CLEAR of the panel floor, out where the leaf's face used to
# be. They are the ghost of the surface that was cut away.
#
# The reason is that `difference` is `max(a, -b)`, so at a point just outside the leaf's own outer
# cylinder, in the middle of a panel, the shell cutter is ALSO just outside itself: it reports
# about -0.006, the field takes the max, and the answer is the distance to a surface that is not
# there any more. That is a legal conservative bound and it is fine — right up until something
# adds 12 mm of noise to it, at which point the sign flips and the vanished surface comes back one
# speckled voxel at a time, floating over the hollow the panel made.
#
# Subtracting the CYLINDER ITSELF instead of a shell of it means the cut region reaches 0.30 m out
# in front of the door. Now the same point reports +0.051 — the real distance to the panel floor —
# and no 12 mm of anything can flip that. Same solid, same arris, same everything you can see.
#
# THE RULE, for anybody sinking a detail into anything: a subtracted region must extend clear of
# the surface it is cut into, never stop flush with it.
#
# The flat side doors below shed nothing, ever, at the same depths — because their cutters are
# plain boxes that happen to start 0.05 in front of the leaf. That is what turned this from a hunch
# into a finding: the control was there to answer "is it the curve?", and the answer was no. It was
# the shell.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ONE PANEL, and the leaf is four of them. 0.90 square — the coffer's own size — with stiles of
# 0.225 (M/2) either side and rails of 0.18 (0.4M) between, which is 4 x 0.90 + 5 x 0.18 = 4.50
# exactly. The row pitch is 1.08, and because the leaf's cylinder stands on y the three copies are
# a plain translate up it and land on the curve without being asked to.
let doors_gd_step  = box 0.225 1.98  -7.95   1.125 2.88  -7.30
let doors_gd_field = box 0.27  2.025 -7.95   1.08  2.835 -7.30
let doors_gd_pat1  = cylinder 0.675 2.43 -7.60 r=0.135 h=1.2 axis=z
let doors_gd_cut1  = union {
    difference { doors_gd_step  doors_gd_cyl_a }
    difference { doors_gd_field doors_gd_cyl_b doors_gd_pat1 }
}
let doors_gd_cut2 = translate { doors_gd_cut1 } 0 1.08 0
let doors_gd_cut3 = translate { doors_gd_cut1 } 0 2.16 0
let doors_gd_cut4 = translate { doors_gd_cut1 } 0 3.24 0

# THE BEAD round the mouth of each panel, 0.045 across and standing 0.045 proud, and it is what
# makes the difference between a bronze door and a brown rectangle. A sunk panel with square sides
# has the SAME NORMAL on its floor as on the stile beside it, so head-on in sun the two shade
# identically and the panel is invisible; only a path trace finds it, by the occlusion in the
# recess. A bead has every normal there is, so it catches a highlight from any direction — which is
# why a real bronze door has one and why this is not decoration.
#
# It has to follow the bow, and over one 0.90 panel the leaf's face moves 0.116 m in z, so a flat
# frame is no use. Four bars, intersected with THE LEAF ITSELF GROWN BY 0.045.
#
# That last part is the whole trick, and it is the fourth and last version of this bead. Grown
# 0.045, the leaf's own field IS the bead's outer surface, offset along its own normal, so the two
# are one expression and cannot disagree about where the door is. Every earlier attempt gave the
# bead a surface of its own — a 0.0675 shell between two cylinders, then a solid cylinder of
# r = 5.445, then r = 5.4525 — and every one of them left exactly one bronze voxel adrift once the
# grain went on, in a different place each time: a surface built parallel to another surface is
# only parallel until something displaces both of them independently.
#
# The bars stop at z = -7.52, which is south of the leaf's own back everywhere under a panel, so
# the bead runs into the door and nothing pokes through into the vestibule behind.
let doors_gd_bead_out = offset { doors_gd_slab } by=0.045
let doors_gd_bead_ring = union {
    box 0.2025 1.9575 -7.95   0.2475 2.9025 -7.52 round=0.012
    box 1.1025 1.9575 -7.95   1.1475 2.9025 -7.52 round=0.012
    box 0.2025 1.9575 -7.95   1.1475 2.0025 -7.52 round=0.012
    box 0.2025 2.8575 -7.95   1.1475 2.9025 -7.52 round=0.012
}
let doors_gd_bead1 = intersection { doors_gd_bead_ring doors_gd_bead_out }
let doors_gd_bead2 = translate { doors_gd_bead1 } 0 1.08 0
let doors_gd_bead3 = translate { doors_gd_bead1 } 0 2.16 0
let doors_gd_bead4 = translate { doors_gd_bead1 } 0 3.24 0

let doors_gd_leaf_e  = union {
    difference { doors_gd_slab doors_gd_cut1 doors_gd_cut2 doors_gd_cut3 doors_gd_cut4 }
    doors_gd_bead1 doors_gd_bead2 doors_gd_bead3 doors_gd_bead4
}
let doors_gd_pair = mirror { doors_gd_leaf_e } axis=x

# The meeting joint: 0.045 wide and 0.045 deep, sunk in the curved skin like the panels, so the
# two leaves read as two without a slot of daylight between them.
let doors_gd_joint = difference {
    box -0.0225 1.75 -7.95   0.0225 6.30 -7.30
    doors_gd_cyl_b
}

# The ring handles, hanging from a boss on the rail, 1.08 m above the floor — where a hand goes.
# A 0.036 tube: one voxel, standing 0.037 clear of a curved surface and biting 0.035 into it.
let doors_gd_boss = cylinder 0.675 2.97 -7.575 r=0.09   h=0.15  axis=z
let doors_gd_ring = torus    0.675 2.88 -7.605 ring=0.1125 tube=0.036 axis=z
let doors_gd_furn = mirror { union { doors_gd_boss doors_gd_ring } } axis=x

let doors_gd_leaves = union {
    difference { doors_gd_pair doors_gd_joint }
    doors_gd_furn
}

let doors_gd_pat2 = translate { doors_gd_pat1 } 0 1.08 0
let doors_gd_pat3 = translate { doors_gd_pat1 } 0 2.16 0
let doors_gd_pat4 = translate { doors_gd_pat1 } 0 3.24 0
let doors_gd_pats = mirror {
    union { doors_gd_pat1 doors_gd_pat2 doors_gd_pat3 doors_gd_pat4 }
} axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE SIDE DOOR — east, and mirrored west. 1.80 (4M) by 3.15 (7M)
# ================================================================================================
#
# x 4.50 .. 6.30, centred on 5.40 — the middle of the portico's outer bay — with the architrave
# running out to 4.05 and 6.75, which are the outer column axes. 3.15 m clear, well over the 2.40
# a door has to give, and this is the door a person actually uses; the great one is ceremonial.

# Two jambs and a head each, unioned, for the reason written over the great door's fasciae.
let doors_sd_f1 = union {
    box 4.32  2.16 -7.545   4.50  5.13 -7.35 round=0.018
    box 6.30  2.16 -7.545   6.48  5.13 -7.35 round=0.018
    box 4.32  4.95 -7.545   6.48  5.13 -7.35 round=0.018
}
let doors_sd_f2 = union {
    box 4.185 2.16 -7.59    4.32  5.265 -7.35 round=0.018
    box 6.48  2.16 -7.59    6.615 5.265 -7.35 round=0.018
    box 4.185 5.13 -7.59    6.615 5.265 -7.35 round=0.018
}
let doors_sd_f3 = union {
    box 4.05  2.16 -7.635   4.185 5.40 -7.35 round=0.018
    box 6.615 2.16 -7.635   6.75  5.40 -7.35 round=0.018
    box 4.05  5.265 -7.635  6.75  5.40 -7.35 round=0.018
}
let doors_sd_ast_plate = union {
    box 4.32   2.16   -7.5675   4.3425 5.13 -7.5675
    box 6.4575 2.16   -7.5675   6.48   5.13 -7.5675
    box 4.32   5.1075 -7.5675   6.48   5.13 -7.5675
}
let doors_sd_astragal = round { doors_sd_ast_plate } by=0.0225

# The hood: a bed ovolo, a corona, a crowning cyma. No pediment — a side door is subordinate, and
# its cornice tops out at 5.85, well under the great door's 8.10.
let doors_sd_bed      = ovolo 3.96 5.49 -7.635   6.84 5.40 -7.725 run=x
let doors_sd_bed_back = box   3.96 5.39 -7.635   6.84 5.49 -7.35 round=0.018
let doors_sd_corona   = box   3.96 5.46 -7.785   6.84 5.76 -7.35 round=0.012
let doors_sd_crown    = cyma  3.96 5.76 -7.785   6.84 5.85 -7.848 run=x
# The stone BEHIND the cyma, and it has to reach INTO the moulding's own rectangle, not stop at the
# edge of it — the same trick `entab_run` uses, and for a reason worth knowing: a cyma is built on
# a subtraction, and a subtraction whose result is then displaced sheds a voxel at the corner where
# nothing stands behind it. One loose voxel of limestone at the tip of this crown was the last
# thing between this part and a single component.
let doors_sd_crn_back = box   3.96 5.73 -7.785   6.84 5.85 -7.35 round=0.012

let doors_sd_case_e = union {
    doors_sd_f1 doors_sd_f2 doors_sd_f3 doors_sd_astragal
    doors_sd_bed doors_sd_bed_back doors_sd_corona doors_sd_crn_back doors_sd_crown
}
let doors_sd_case = mirror { doors_sd_case_e } axis=x

# --- the leaves: FLAT, and that is the point (see the load note at the top) ---------------------
#
# Set 0.09 behind the wall face and 0.135 behind the architrave, 0.09 (0.2M) thick, lapping 0.045
# behind each jamb and 0.05 into the threshold. Three panels of 0.54 x 0.81 to a leaf:
# 3 x 0.81 + 4 x 0.18 = 3.15, and 0.18 + 0.54 + 0.18 = 0.90. Same first step, same floor, same
# patera, same bead as the great door's — everything the same except the curve.
let doors_sd_slab = box 4.455 1.75 -7.41   6.345 4.95 -7.32 round=0.012

# The cutters start at z = -7.46, which is 0.05 IN FRONT of the leaf's face — see the note on the
# great door's panels for why that 0.05 is the difference between a door and a cloud of bronze.
let doors_sd_step_a  = box 4.68  1.98  -7.46   5.22  2.79  -7.3875
let doors_sd_step_b  = box 5.58  1.98  -7.46   6.12  2.79  -7.3875
let doors_sd_field_a = box 4.725 2.025 -7.46   5.175 2.745 -7.365
let doors_sd_field_b = box 5.625 2.025 -7.46   6.075 2.745 -7.365
let doors_sd_pat_a   = cylinder 4.95 2.385 -7.60 r=0.09 h=1.2 axis=z
let doors_sd_pat_b   = cylinder 5.85 2.385 -7.60 r=0.09 h=1.2 axis=z

let doors_sd_pats1 = union { doors_sd_pat_a doors_sd_pat_b }
let doors_sd_cut1  = union {
    doors_sd_step_a doors_sd_step_b
    difference { union { doors_sd_field_a doors_sd_field_b } doors_sd_pats1 }
}
let doors_sd_cut2 = translate { doors_sd_cut1 } 0 0.99 0
let doors_sd_cut3 = translate { doors_sd_cut1 } 0 1.98 0
let doors_sd_joint = box 5.3775 1.75 -7.46   5.4225 4.95 -7.365

# The same bead, and on a flat leaf it is just eight bars — nothing to offset, because the face
# does not move. Standing 0.045 proud of the leaf and biting 0.045 into it.
let doors_sd_bead1 = union {
    box 4.6575 1.9575 -7.455   4.7025 2.8125 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 5.1975 1.9575 -7.455   5.2425 2.8125 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 4.6575 1.9575 -7.455   5.2425 2.0025 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 4.6575 2.7675 -7.455   5.2425 2.8125 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 5.5575 1.9575 -7.455   5.6025 2.8125 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 6.0975 1.9575 -7.455   6.1425 2.8125 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 5.5575 1.9575 -7.455   6.1425 2.0025 -7.365 round=0.012
    box 5.5575 2.7675 -7.455   6.1425 2.8125 -7.365 round=0.012
}
let doors_sd_bead2 = translate { doors_sd_bead1 } 0 0.99 0
let doors_sd_bead3 = translate { doors_sd_bead1 } 0 1.98 0

let doors_sd_leaf_e = union {
    difference { doors_sd_slab doors_sd_cut1 doors_sd_cut2 doors_sd_cut3 doors_sd_joint }
    doors_sd_bead1 doors_sd_bead2 doors_sd_bead3
}
let doors_sd_leaves = mirror { doors_sd_leaf_e } axis=x

let doors_sd_pats2 = translate { doors_sd_pats1 } 0 0.99 0
let doors_sd_pats3 = translate { doors_sd_pats1 } 0 1.98 0
let doors_sd_pats  = mirror {
    union { doors_sd_pats1 doors_sd_pats2 doors_sd_pats3 }
} axis=x

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let doors_stone  = union { doors_plinth doors_gd_case doors_sd_case }
let doors_leaves = union { doors_gd_leaves doors_sd_leaves }

let part_doors = union { doors_stone doors_leaves }

# What is taken out of the building: every opening, MINUS the leaves standing in it. The manifest
# subtracts this from everything after everything is assembled, so writing the leaves back into
# the void here is what leaves them standing in a hole that goes clean through the wall — and it
# guarantees the sunk panels stay sunk however thick somebody else's wall turns out to be.
#
# 1.80 m of northward punch (4M) is more than any wall this building can have; 0.45 southward is
# air under the portico.
let doors_gd_void   = box -1.35 1.80 -7.95   1.35 6.30 -5.70
let doors_sd_void_e = box  4.50 1.80 -7.95   6.30 4.95 -5.70
let doors_sd_void   = mirror { doors_sd_void_e } axis=x

let void_doors = difference {
    union { doors_gd_void doors_sd_void }
    doors_leaves
}

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT — only my own shapes, always below=0.02
# ================================================================================================

paint marble  where=doors_stone  below=0.02
paint granite where=doors_plinth below=0.02

# The tympanum in porphyry. A marble doorcase on a limestone wall is two pale stones four points
# apart and in sun the pediment simply vanishes — which is what the contact sheet of this part
# standing in the finished portico showed, and the reason this line exists. A dark panel inside the
# gable is also exactly what the thing is for: it is the one place on a Roman front where coloured
# stone is used, and it gives the path tracer a saturated red bouncing up into a white raking
# cornice 0.27 m in front of it.
paint porphyry where=doors_ped_tympanum below=0.02

paint bronze  where=doors_leaves below=0.02

# The paterae last, so they win over the bronze under them. Clipped to the leaves so a cylinder
# that runs on through the reveal cannot colour anybody else's stone.
let doors_paterae = intersection { union { doors_gd_pats doors_sd_pats } doors_leaves }
paint gilt where=doors_paterae below=0.02
